"Mr. López's fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. López need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?","Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?",7,7,7,7,"Step 1: Determine total glue sticks needed.
- There are 27 students, each needs 2 glue sticks.
- Total needed = 27 × 2 = 54 glue sticks.

Step 2: Determine how many packs are needed if each pack has 8 glue sticks.
- Packs needed = 54 ÷ 8 = 6.75 packs.

Step 3: Since Mr. López can only buy whole packs, round up to the next whole number.
- He needs 7 packs.

Step 4: (Optional check) Extra glue sticks:
- 7 packs × 8 = 56 glue sticks.
- Extra = 56 − 54 = 2 glue sticks.

